{"id":"fast-rising-classes-holders","title":"Classes whose scale outran the general increases: who holds them, and what it costs","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"For the job classes whose published top step rose far faster than the typical class, how many people hold them and what does the extra rise cost a year?","finding":"Across 972 job classes the top published step rose 48.3% between fiscal 2014-15 and fiscal 2025-26 in the typical class; 72 classes rose at least ten points more. 65 of those had paid holders in fiscal 2026: 4,877 people with regular salaries of $430.8 million. Had those classes risen only as fast as the typical class, the salary bill would be about $39.6 million a year lower. Most of that is Transit Operator (class 9163) (2,528 holders, top step up 63%, about $18.5 million a year), IS Business Analyst-Principal (class 1054) (182, $4.0 million) and Parking Control Officer (class 8214) (352, $2.9 million).","how":"The classes are found as in the screen “Job classes whose top pay rose far faster than the rest” under waivers and exceptions: the highest step of each class in the first and last year of the City’s table of hourly pay by class and step, against the middle rise across all classes. This table adds what that screen leaves out: the people paid regular salary in each class in fiscal 2026, their salary, and the part of it that corresponds to the rise above the typical class (salary multiplied by one minus the typical growth over the class’s growth). Ranked by that sum.","limits":"The cost figure is an estimate of size, not a saving on offer: it assumes every holder is paid in proportion to the top step, and it says nothing about whether the rise was needed to fill the jobs. Classes paid on ranges, such as managers, are not in the step table. The labour agreements and Civil Service Commission records would show why each class moved.","columns":[{"k":"job","t":"Job class, title as in the pay table","type":"text"},{"k":"departments","t":"Main depa